# Mobile-first density — Hannah's defaults

Hannah designs a mobile-first field app for CP Build. She works on phones
first, desktops second. When she asks for UI, apply these defaults in the
FIRST draft — don't wait for her to push you toward compactness.

Full rationale and examples: `docs/design/MOBILE_DENSITY.md`.

## 1. Icons beat labels for secondary actions

Any button that is NOT the primary CTA on the screen is **icon-only** by
default. Put the label in `aria-label` and `title`.

| Action            | Default              |
|-------------------|----------------------|
| Preview           | `<Eye />` only       |
| Back / close      | `<ArrowLeft />` / `<X />` only |
| Overflow menu     | `<MoreHorizontal />` only     |
| Save (secondary)  | `<Save />` only      |
| Duplicate         | `<Copy />` only      |
| Delete            | `<Trash2 />` only    |
| Expand / collapse | chevron only         |

Keep a label only on:

- The **single primary CTA** on the screen (Publish, Submit, Continue).
- Actions where the icon is genuinely ambiguous AND the screen has space.
- Destructive confirmations inside menus (where an icon alone could mislead).

If you're tempted to put labels on two sibling buttons, one of them should
be an icon.

## 2. Autosave replaces explicit Save

If a surface edits persistent data (forms, settings, profile, etc.), prefer
autosave with a lightweight "Saved / Saving…" indicator over a dedicated
Save button. If you keep an explicit Save button alongside autosave
(because users expect it), it should be **icon-only** — the text-label Save
is the one that gets cut on narrow screens.

## 3. Header toolbars: the 3-item rule

Sticky top bars on mobile hold at most:

- 1 navigation control (back / close)
- 1 contextual indicator (status pill, saved state, step counter)
- 2–3 actions, with AT MOST ONE text label (the primary CTA)

Everything else goes into an overflow (`⋯`) menu or a secondary surface.

If your toolbar has more than 3 buttons OR more than 1 labeled button,
stop and cut.

## 4. Tap targets come from padding, not label length

Mobile hit targets are 40–44 px minimum — achieve that with padding
**around** small icons, not by oversizing labels.

- A 16 px icon inside a 32×32 or 40×40 ghost button ✅
- A 48 px wide "Save draft" pill next to another labeled button ❌

## 5. Spacing defaults — these are MAXIMUMS on mobile, not targets

When you write a card/row/section for the first time, start from the LOW end
of these ranges. Larger values require a specific reason (desktop-only
surface, accessibility tap target, designer asked for breathing room).

- Header height: 52–56 px (one finger tap)
- Toolbar horizontal padding: **10–12 px mobile**, 20–24 px desktop
- Card padding: **10–12 px mobile**, 16–20 px desktop
- List/page wrapper padding around a stack of cards: **10–12 px mobile**,
  16–24 px desktop — do NOT double-pad (if the page wrapper has 16 px
  horizontal padding and each card has 16 px, that's 32 px of chrome
  before content)
- Row padding: 8–12 px vertical on mobile — never more
- Gaps between toolbar items: 6–8 px
- Gaps between stacked cards: 8 px mobile, 12 px desktop
- Flex gap inside a card row (icon + content + actions): 8–10 px, never 14+

**Red flag:** if you catch yourself writing `padding: "16px"` or larger on
a card, row, or list wrapper without a specific reason, you're defaulting
to desktop comfort values. Drop to 10–12 px.

## 6. The 320 px test

Before handing UI to Hannah: imagine the screen at 320 px wide (iPhone SE
portrait). What wraps, overflows, or crowds out content? Fix it BEFORE
showing her. Common offenders:

- Labeled buttons in sticky headers
- Icon + label combos when icon alone would do
- Large padding on cards / rows
- Nested indentation eating width

## 7. Never nest padded containers on mobile

On mobile breakpoints, **never** use nested padded boxes to show hierarchy.
Every level of nesting eats ~20–30 px of horizontal width (left + right
padding) on screens that are already only 320–400 px wide. By level 3
you've surrendered ~90 px — roughly 25 % of the viewport — to chrome.

**Forbidden patterns on mobile:**

```
[card p=16 [callout p=14 [inner-card p=12 [content]]]]   ❌
[section p=16 [sub-section p=14 [row p=10 [content]]]]   ❌
```

**Use one of these instead:**

- **Flatten a level**: replace `[card [inner-card [content]]]` with a
  single card and separate items with 1 px top borders, gaps, or subtle
  background tints. The colored container IS the visual grouping — no
  need for a second padded box inside it.
- **Bleed to edges**: use negative horizontal margin on the inner block
  (`marginLeft: -14, marginRight: -14, paddingLeft: 14, paddingRight: 14`)
  when emphasis is needed. This costs zero width.
- **Drop the wrapper**: if the only job of an inner container is visual
  grouping, delete it and use spacing or color only.

**Rule of thumb:** at 320 px wide, your content should never sit inside
more than **two** padded containers (the page, and one visual section).
If you need a third level, you're designing for desktop — stop and
refactor.

## 8. Reserve space for action buttons — don't float them over content

When a card or row has action buttons (edit, preview, overflow menu, etc.)
in one corner, they must be rendered as a **flex sibling** of the
content column — NOT absolute-positioned on top of it. Absolute
positioning guarantees that long titles, descriptions, or status pills
will eventually flow underneath the icons, especially on narrow screens.

**Wrong (content can overlap actions):**

```
<div style={{ position: "relative", padding: 14 }}>
  <div>{title}{description}{meta}</div>
  <div style={{ position: "absolute", top: 12, right: 14 }}>
    [Eye] [⋯]
  </div>
</div>
```

**Right (content column and actions are siblings, no overlap possible):**

```
<div style={{ display: "flex", gap: 8, padding: 12 }}>
  <div style={{ flex: 1, minWidth: 0 }}>{title}{description}{meta}</div>
  <div style={{ flexShrink: 0, position: "relative" }}>
    [Eye] [⋯]
    {menuOpen && <Dropdown />}  {/* position:absolute relative to actions wrapper */}
  </div>
</div>
```

`minWidth: 0` on the content column is required — without it, the title
tries to take its intrinsic width and shoves the actions off-screen
instead of wrapping. The actions wrapper keeps `position: relative` so
any dropdown menus inside it still anchor correctly.

**Only use absolute positioning for:** ribbons/badges that intentionally
bleed over the card border (e.g. a "Just published" ribbon on the top
edge), or decorative overlays that must sit outside the flex flow.

## 9. No decorative icons on repeating list items

A list of N items that all share the same type (e.g. a list of forms,
a list of projects) does NOT need an identical icon on every row. The
page title and context already establish what kind of thing these are.
Putting a 40×40 icon blob on each card burns ~54 px of horizontal
width per row (icon + gap) that could be content, and adds zero
signal because the icon is constant.

**Only include a per-item icon when it conveys real per-item information:**

- Unit type color chips on the Locations page ✅ (the color varies)
- File-type icon on a mixed list of docs/images/forms ✅ (the icon varies)
- A clipboard icon on every form in a list of forms ❌ (constant, redundant)

When you catch yourself reaching for `<Icon />` inside a repeating card,
stop and ask: does this icon change from row to row? If not, delete it.

## 10. When in doubt

Fewer elements, more compact spacing, icons over labels, flatter
hierarchy. Hannah would rather you delete a button than add one.

